ABOUT THIS FORK
================================================================

This repository is a fork of CSIRO's Delivery code
(https://github.com/csiro/Delivery_Seismic_Welltie_CSEM).

It adds an AI-assisted rewrite of the seismic inverter ("delivery") in
Python, in the python/ folder. The rewrite was made with Claude (Anthropic)
and checked against the original Java code on its example problems. It keeps
Delivery's discrete geology (fluid states, fluid contacts, pinch-outs), runs
without Java or Seismic Unix, and reads and writes HDF5, NumPy (.npz) and
SEG-Y files.

  python/README.md                        how to install and run it
  python/examples/synthetic_wedge.ipynb   step-by-step tutorial notebook
  python/examples/fluid_contacts.ipynb    gas/oil/brine contacts with known truth

The wavelet extractor and the CSEM codes are not yet ported. The original
Java, C and Fortran code below is unchanged, and its README follows.
Delivery and its method are the work of James Gunning (CSIRO / BHP
Billiton); licence terms are as in the original (GPL).

================================================================


INTRODUCTION
---------------------------------------------------------------- 

This is the main README for the open source trace-based Bayesian Seismic
inversion code called "delivery" and its companion "waveletExtractor" and
"deliveryMassager" codes.

The theoretical framework for delivery and its companions is described in the
Computers and Geosciences preprints/papers at
Docs/*.pdf
where the 3 papers are:
 
DeliveryPaper.pdf 
WaveletExtraction.pdf
DeliveryMassager.pdf

which ought to be digested before attempting to run the code.

The source tree includes some basic examples illustrating the use of the
code, usually on small data sets.


+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
FOR EXISTING DELIVERY USERS ONLY:

    Some small schema changes have occurred in the XSD files. Make sure you
    use the new schemas. Old Delivery XML files can be converted to new format
	using scripts/XMLconverter.sh
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 

SETUP
________________________________________________________________

The delivery inversion suite is most naturally suited to a Linux/Unix environment.
It interfaces most naturally with Seismic Unix (SU) (http://timna.mines.edu/cwpcodes/),
and familiarity with these codes is expected. People have reported success with MacOSX,
and CYGWIN on Windows too. THE BHP_SU extensions to SU are useful to have 
installed, but not mandatory.

To set up "delivery" codes,

(1) Ensure you have a java 1.5 or later installed (must have a jit or hotspot)
(2) Ensure the scripts directory is in your $PATH
(3) Read and edit scripts/environment_setup.sh to reflect your setup
(4) The 5 main scripts you want to use are 
       delivery
       deliveryAnalyser
       waveletExtractor
       deliveryMassager.sh   (script for native binary)
       xmledit.sh
    The top 4 are like SU programs and self-document.
    xmledit.sh fires up a GUI style XML editor for constructing/editing parameter
    files. For CYGWIN users, a small edit to these files is required (see the CYGWIN 
    comments in the scripts).

    DeliveryMassager (called deliveryMassager.sh) is a native code
    that must be compiled in general.
    % cd NativeCodes
    and follow the README_* files.

Graphics
________

Many of the examples use convenient plotting utilities for X windows
capable of taking ascii or binary input to illustrate what is going on. You'll need
something of this flavour in order to understand what the inversions are doing.
SU has some good plotting utilities ones (e.g. "xgraph"), but I tend
to use GNU's plotutils package, especially the "graph" program. This is
free & easy to find for any *nix system, but use whatever you like. The example
READMEs will show lines where ascii data is piped into "graph", so you can
substitute your favourite at the obvious place.


EXAMPLES
________________________________________________________________

(1) Delivery (Inversion)

In the au/csiro/JamesGunning/Tests/InverterTests
directory are a bunch of test problems, usually with
READMEs in each directory.

The model is controlled by a master XML file, and a set of traces which
provide seismic data and prior information (that may vary on a trace-by trace basis)
To play with the model, open an xml file with the xmleditor, and look in
the hierarchy to see what's there. Double & right clicking enables you to edit
and add things (see the editor help facility).
Eg.

% cd au/csiro/JamesGunning/Tests/InverterTests/1TraceFluidDetection
% xmledit.sh xml=ModelDescription.xml



Here's a brief summary.


1TraceFluidDetection   3 layer problem (shale/reservoir/shale) aimed at fluid detection. 
                       Discussed in the C&G preprint.

3LayerBHPWedge         3 layer problem with pinchout. Test problem for nasty multimodality/loop skipping

3LayerWedge            3 layer problem with pinchout, smaller & faster than BHP problem.
                       Discussed in the C&G preprint.  

GasOilFlank            2 layer problem (shale/reservoir) testing fluid detection vs velocity uncertainty
                       To set up simple "fluid detection from single peak-amplitude" type models, this is probably
                       a good suite of files to extrapolate from.

IsopachTests           Tests of isopach constraints

NG_Wedge               Test of detection of trends in the net-to-gross in a reservoir layer (no fluid effects)
                       Discussed in the C&G preprint.

Simple3LayerContactsTestProblem  
					   Example case of fluid contacts presenting major reflections within layers, and
                       how to constrain these problems with known depth contacts.

Experimental           6 layer single-trace problem testing many exotic features of inverter.
                       Mainly for development testing


----------------------------------------------------------------

(2) Wavelet Extraction

In the au/csiro/JamesGunning/Tests/WaveletExtractorTests
are the examples discussed in the WaveletExtraction.pdf paper

----------------------------------------------------------------

(3) DeliveryMassager

In the au/csiro/JamesGunning/Tests/MassagerTests are the
(non-proprietary) examples discussed in the DeliveryMassager.pdf
paper





REBUILDING/HACKING
__________________________________________________________________

(1) java code (Delivery, WaveletExtractor)

To rebuild the java code: your system must have "make", which
is usually standard in Linux & unix. This should not be necessary.

% cd au/csiro/JamesGunning/
% make

There is some JNI material in
au/csiro/JamesGunning/lib/Numerical/djbFFT/
for which native libraries on Linux (intel 32 & 64 bit) & OSX
have been built. Porting these should not be too hard. 
The java code has fallback java methods in any case. These
JNI libraries are provided in order to get optimally fast
FFTs.

To access these native libraries, you should append (e.g. on intel-linux)
$SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME/au/csiro/JamesGunning/lib/Numerical/djbFFT/djbfft-0.76/Linux-i686-unknown/lib
to the standard library search path 
Linux: LD_LIBRARY_PATH
OSX: JAVA_LIBRARY_PATH 
where $SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME is the 
root of your delivery installation.

E.g. Linux:
(tcsh)
% setenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH $SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME/au/csiro/JamesGunning/lib/Numerical/djbFFT/djbfft-0.76/${CSIRO_OSTYPE}/lib
(bash)
% export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME/au/csiro/JamesGunning/lib/Numerical/djbFFT/djbfft-0.76/${CSIRO_OSTYPE}/lib

E.g. OSX:
(tcsh)
% setenv JAVA_LIBRARY_PATH $SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME/au/csiro/JamesGunning/lib/Numerical/djbFFT/djbfft-0.76/${CSIRO_OSTYPE}/lib
(bash)
% export JAVA_LIBRARY_PATH=$SEISMIC_INVERTER_HOME/au/csiro/JamesGunning/lib/Numerical/djbFFT/djbfft-0.76/${CSIRO_OSTYPE}/lib

(2) Native codes (DeliveryMassager)

You need the usual c compilers etc.

% cd NativeCodes 

and follow the README_*

